// NEGATIVE TEST — a range pattern's endpoints are integers (N6-03).
//
// The normative production spells an endpoint `expression`, which no pattern
// position in this compiler can evaluate; what a range needs is an ORDER, and
// the only ordered literal this language has today is the integer. `char` is the
// obvious second one and waits on N4-04, which owes `char` as a type at all —
// so a `char` endpoint cannot be checked against a scrutinee type that does not
// exist.
//
// Refused with the offending end named, rather than lowered into a C comparison
// between string pointers, which compares addresses and would answer.
fn main() {
    let v = match 4 {
        "a".."z" => 1,
        _ => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
